 * Firebase entry point: the 540 design system MCP as an HTTP Cloud Function (2nd gen).
 * Firebase Hosting rewrites /mcp to it (firebase.json). It is bundled with its metadata into
 * firebase/functions/ by scripts/build-mcp-firebase.ts; never deployed from this file directly.
 */
import { onRequest } from 'firebase-functions/v2/https';
import metadata from './metadata.json' with { type: 'json' };
import { handleMcpRequest } from './http.js';
import { sendWebResponse, toWebRequest } from './node-adapter.js';

export const mcp = onRequest(
  {
    region: 'europe-west1',
    /** Public: Claude calls it without a Google login. Read-only, and its content is public. */
    invoker: 'public',
    memory: '256MiB',
    /** No idle instances: free when unused, at the cost of a cold start after a quiet spell. */
    minInstances: 0,
    /** The spending cap: with 80 requests per instance, three instances serve 240 at once. More than that is abuse, not use. */
    maxInstances: 3,
    concurrency: 80,
    timeoutSeconds: 30,
  },
  async (req, res) => {
    const request = toWebRequest(req, `https://${req.hostname}`, req.rawBody);
    await sendWebResponse(res, await handleMcpRequest(request, metadata));
  },
);
{"body":"## Why\n\nThe MCP server's HTTP transport — mcp/http.ts (handleMcpRequest), mcp/server.ts (createServer) and mcp/node-adapter.ts (toWebRequest, sendWebResponse) — was documented by no capability: 'predictable specs undocumented' lists them among 134 exported functions no capability documents, and the only capability, SPEC-263 MCP tools, covers mcp/tools and mcp/piece-types.ts. The bridge between Node and Web requests also had no test at all.\n\n## What changed\n\n- New capability SPEC-264 'MCP transports', covering mcp/http.ts, mcp/server.ts and mcp/node-adapter.ts. Its description states only what the code does, checked against it: the stateless POST-only handler (OPTIONS 204, other methods 405 with Allow: POST, OPTIONS, no session id, plain JSON, open CORS on every answer), createServer's name, instructions and read-only tools, and how toWebRequest/sendWebResponse carry method, URL, headers and body.\n- New test mcp/node-adapter.test.ts (4 tests) for toWebRequest (method, URL against origin, repeated headers, no body on GET, rawBody vs streamed body) and sendWebResponse (status, headers, whole body). The handler and server statements are covered by the existing mcp/http.test.ts and mcp/server.test.ts.\n- Verified: npx tsc -p tsconfig.mcp.json --noEmit, npm run test:mcp (36 passed), predictable sync.\n- Not in scope: mcp/serve.ts, mcp/stdio.ts and mcp/firebase.ts (entry points that start servers; not stated, since no test covers them). The 28 violated requirements sync reports are all derived from analysis findings (defects), none written by a person; this PR changes no code.\n\n## How it was verified\n\nNot verified by the analysis: no `predictable fix-check` in this session reported a defect gone for this change. Review it as an unverified change.\n\n<!-- predictable-code:auto-merge off method=squash -->\n","state":"MERGED","title":"docs(specs): MCP transports capability"}
